comments.body:
@reporter does the leak happen with #12092 and #12114 applied? The idmapped code was very fragile before those two PRs IMHO, hitting some concurrency issues that you point out. It's not obvious why it was happening, but I do wonder now that the logic is way simpler with those two PRs (and the cleanup functions should work fine), if it still happens.

I guess if it happens, it might be slower to accumulate mounts (as we are doing only one mount instead of potentially a lot per rootfs), but I'm curious to know if it still happens.

If it happens, I think we need to debug to understand why. Furthermore, containerd is adding a garbage collector and mounts that we couldn't clean at the right time is a clear candidate for that.

@participant4 yeah, a mount leaking while we don't want it, it shouldn't be that bad. The gc won't restore the free space until the mount is gone.

workarounds to remove the mount: restart or a manual unmount should do it.

Just curious, are you using overlayfs or another snapshotter? (the default is overlay)

@participant2 thanks for investigating! I've tried using PR #11912 so systemd doesn't see this mount and it didn't make a difference (I mentioned it on that commit message, in PR #12114). Now that I think about it, though, I'm not sure it's relevant as I think this mount might not be made by the shim but by the main process. But I'm not sure.

Anyways, this happens with all temp mounts as @reporter mentioned [here][link], so using the helper that other temp mounts use to retry the unmount seems fine. We can later try to see if we can avoid the unmount for ever failing due to containerd behavior, but there can easily be other host processes monitoring the mounts and therefore we will still need to keep the retry IMO.

@participant2 what do you think?

[link]: https://github.com/containerd/containerd/issues/12139#issuecomment-3165535459
